adjudication.rationale:
The diff changes `EventManager` to treat `destinationCalendar` as an array:
```ts
const [mainHostDestinationCalendar] = evt.destinationCalendar ?? [];
if (evt.location === MeetLocationType && mainHostDestinationCalendar.integration !== "google_calendar") {
  evt["location"] = "integrations:daily";
}
```
If `evt.destinationCalendar` is `undefined`, `null`, or an empty array (which the diff explicitly introduces in multiple places, e.g. `bookingReminder.ts` sets `destinationCalendar: selectedDestinationCalendar ? [selectedDestinationCalendar] : []`, and the Playwright test changes `destinationCalendar` to `[]`), then `mainHostDestinationCalendar` will be `undefined`. Accessing `mainHostDestinationCalendar.integration` will throw a `TypeError` at runtime. Optional chaining (e.g. `mainHostDestinationCalendar?.integration`) or a guard is needed. This is a real null/empty-safety regression introduced by the diff.

In packages/core/EventManager.ts the diff introduces:

```ts
const [mainHostDestinationCalendar] = evt.destinationCalendar ?? [];
if (evt.location === MeetLocationType && mainHostDestinationCalendar.integration !== "google_calendar") {
  ...
}
```

If `evt.destinationCalendar` is `null`, `undefined`, or an empty array, destructuring assigns `undefined` to `mainHostDestinationCalendar`. Accessing `mainHostDestinationCalendar.integration` will then throw a `TypeError`. This matches the proposed finding (Google Meet fallback path with no destination calendar). The diff does not guarantee `destinationCalendar` is always non-empty at this point, only that it's an array type now.
